<h1>binomial tree</h1>
<p>
(data structure)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
An <a href="orderedtree.html"><em>ordered tree</em></a> of <a href="order.html"><em>order</em></a> k &ge; 0, that is B<sub>k</sub>, whose <a href="root.html"><em>root</em></a> has k <a href="child.html"><em>children</em></a> where the i<sup>th</sup> child is binomial tree of order k-i.
</p>

<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="binomialheap.html"><em>binomial heap</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
A B<sub>k</sub> tree has 2<sup>k</sup> nodes, the <a href="height.html"><em>height</em></a> is k, and there are k choose i nodes at depth i. </p> 
<p> Adapted from <a href="../terms.html#CLR90">[CLR90, pages 401 and 402]</a>. CLR90 numbers the children k-1, k-2, ..., 0, making child i a binomial tree of order i. This definition numbers the children from 1 to k.  </em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>
<h2>More information</h2>
<p>
<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binomial_heap" target="_blank">Binomial heap</a> in Wikipedia.
</p>

